      <data key="k493">Propose short cycles: examine the existing behavior, clarify and version the next intention delta, analyze design impact, implement one reversible slice, independently test, review, deploy and learn. Repository studies motivate the need for bounded changes, while FlowGen compares simulated Scrum/TDD/Waterfall processes on coding benchmarks—not real agile brownfield team outcomes.</data>
      <data key="k520" />
    </node>
    <node id="S:4.7">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":["C:C04"],"authors":"","category":"sections","collection":null,"id":"S:4.7","kind":"section","label":"Late intentions and explicit impact analysis","limitation":"","mapped":["C:C04"],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"When a new regional requirement arrives after code exists, retain the previous intent revision; record who authorized the change, which design alternatives and checks it invalidates, and whether to amend, roll back or defer the implementation. Code-critique and OSS-policy observations motivate traceability; the delta/impact workflow is a proposed method rather than a measured benefit of those studies.","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":[],"year":""}</data>
