      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"Gergely Orosz","category":null,"collection":"P","id":"P052","kind":"practitioner_article","label":"Cursor makes developers less effective?","limitation":"P052 says 136 issues and implies assignment by developer. METR’s primary methodology reports 246 issues and issue-level randomization, with developers working in both conditions. Do not reproduce the secondary account’s conflicting design description. A primary abstract/methodology check was performed, not a full-paper audit or statistical replication. This is a retitled Pulse excerpt; preserve its existing series provenance. Public availability does not imply an independent check of every paragraph or chart. No microdata replication, controlled product test, or independent-rater agreement study was performed.","mapped":["C:C02"],"printed":true,"streams":["T:Productivity"],"summary":"Discusses METR’s experienced-maintainer experiment and possible workflow explanations.","topics":["K:Controlled productivity evidence","K:Evaluation methodology and baselines"],"url":"https://blog.pragmaticengineer.com/cursor-makes-developers-less-effective/","volumes":[],"year":"2025-07-24"}</data>
