    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0029">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0029","kind":"cited_passage","label":"A code completion tool suggests text within a human-directed edit. An agentic coding system can read repository files, decide which files to inspect next, call ","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">A code completion tool suggests text within a human-directed edit. An agentic coding system can read repository files, decide which files to inspect next, call </data>
      <data key="k263">13</data>
      <data key="k265">13</data>
      <data key="k306">A code completion tool suggests text within a human-directed edit. An agentic coding system can read repository files, decide which files to inspect next, call tools, apply patches and react to the resulting outputs. That greater delegation changes the system\textquotesingle s exposure to untrusted material. A README, dependency script or test output can influence the agent\textquotesingle s next action even though none has authority to issue operational instructions. Liu and colleagues\textquotesingle{} study of prompt injection in coding editors \autocite{W060} is directly relevant to that boundary, while their production-scale trace study \autocite{W012} provides a route for observing how tool-using systems actually behave. The precise attack rates and trace counts are not asserted here because their original result tables have not been verified in this draft.</data>
      <data key="k446">e3f1cf90724c8c17ba0d73ee2577df258e766d9238152155336392813a8bc421</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter03.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W012", "W060"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0030">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0030","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Chen and colleagues\\textquotesingle{} study of increasing automation \\autocite{W002} examines how the distribution of developer activity changes as AI does more","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Chen and colleagues\textquotesingle{} study of increasing automation \autocite{W002} examines how the distribution of developer activity changes as AI does more</data>
      <data key="k263">28</data>
      <data key="k265">28</data>
      <data key="k306">Chen and colleagues\textquotesingle{} study of increasing automation \autocite{W002} examines how the distribution of developer activity changes as AI does more of the work. Ehsani and colleagues\textquotesingle{} analysis of failed agentic pull requests \autocite{W191} focuses attention on observable failure outcomes. The two sources address different levels of analysis: a human workflow can feel more automated while a specific proposed patch remains unacceptable. We should not infer failure frequencies from the bibliography\textquotesingle s short annotations alone.</data>
      <data key="k446">4a0ccc1c42b4dcd12a27201078384277e9d29e18e749278797002ac65ea3543a</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter03.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W002", "W191"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0031">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0031","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Pinna\\textquotesingle s task-stratified study of coding-agent pull-request acceptance \\autocite{W034} and Ehsani\\textquotesingle s failed-PR analysis \\autocite{","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Pinna\textquotesingle s task-stratified study of coding-agent pull-request acceptance \autocite{W034} and Ehsani\textquotesingle s failed-PR analysis \autocite{</data>
      <data key="k263">36</data>
      <data key="k265">36</data>
      <data key="k306">Pinna\textquotesingle s task-stratified study of coding-agent pull-request acceptance \autocite{W034} and Ehsani\textquotesingle s failed-PR analysis \autocite{W191} are relevant to deciding which tasks warrant supervision. MirrorCode \autocite{W193} challenges simplistic assumptions that long calendar duration automatically makes a task impossible for AI, but its demonstrated capability does not supply a production reliability guarantee for arbitrary multi-week changes. Nor does a high success rate on selected benchmark tasks imply that an agent should control a high-consequence policy change.</data>
      <data key="k446">d410951a2a6fd046ae10241f88043b047ca3adb3df54ee1f5af558eee9e39359</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter03.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W034", "W191", "W193"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0032">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0032","kind":"cited_passage","label":"\\noindent\\textbf{Two dimensions of delegation.} Osmani's practitioner framework separates \\emph{agency}---how independently one agent can plan and act---from \\e","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">\noindent\textbf{Two dimensions of delegation.} Osmani's practitioner framework separates \emph{agency}---how independently one agent can plan and act---from \e</data>
      <data key="k263">43</data>
      <data key="k265">43</data>
      <data key="k306">\noindent\textbf{Two dimensions of delegation.} Osmani's practitioner framework separates \emph{agency}---how independently one agent can plan and act---from \emph{orchestration}---how many agents can be coordinated, isolated and reconciled \osmanicite{O010}. His six illustrative levels describe a possible operating progression, not a validated organizational maturity scale. Keeping the axes separate makes the task-envelope rule more precise. One agent with permission to alter transaction eligibility has high-impact authority even in a single worktree. Several agents inspecting documentation in isolated, read-only workspaces can require more coordination without gaining authority to change policy.</data>
      <data key="k446">f4cd41aaebf575836f1ab4902f00f6fd3610351c4a40f30e863921480abf2e11</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter03.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["O010"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0033">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0033","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Ehsani\\textquotesingle s failed-PR study \\autocite{W191}, Peralta\\textquotesingle s analysis of why agentic pull requests are merged or rejected \\autocite{W071}","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Ehsani\textquotesingle s failed-PR study \autocite{W191}, Peralta\textquotesingle s analysis of why agentic pull requests are merged or rejected \autocite{W071}</data>
      <data key="k263">74</data>
      <data key="k265">74</data>
      <data key="k306">Ehsani\textquotesingle s failed-PR study \autocite{W191}, Peralta\textquotesingle s analysis of why agentic pull requests are merged or rejected \autocite{W071}, and Tie\textquotesingle s work on software-engineering pitfalls \autocite{W158} provide relevant research directions. In this chapter we do not attach invented proportions to those categories: their detailed coding schemes and tables must be checked in the source manuscripts first. The practical value is the failure taxonomy, which makes each rejection actionable.</data>
      <data key="k446">e549a5740b21e89e4595e78bba8dff7066b4fb0a31bb1c2da21aabe96ee829f1</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter03.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W071", "W158", "W191"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0034">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0034","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Production trace research \\autocite{W012} helps characterize actual tool-use patterns, and failed-PR research \\autocite{W191} motivates explicit failure exits. ","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Production trace research \autocite{W012} helps characterize actual tool-use patterns, and failed-PR research \autocite{W191} motivates explicit failure exits. </data>
      <data key="k263">82</data>
      <data key="k265">82</data>
      <data key="k306">Production trace research \autocite{W012} helps characterize actual tool-use patterns, and failed-PR research \autocite{W191} motivates explicit failure exits. Qiu\textquotesingle s blueprint-first deterministic workflow \autocite{W151} provides a cross-domain architectural analogy for separating policy from model execution; it is not direct evidence that this exact loop improves brownfield coding outcomes. The loop described here is an author-proposed method, and its effectiveness must be evaluated locally.</data>
      <data key="k446">c3ec3104c941ca09dc6696bda11c948b9379558873283a37cd91cacf5866da4d</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter03.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W012", "W151", "W191"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0035">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0035","kind":"cited_passage","label":"\\noindent\\textbf{What turns repeated prompting into an engineered loop?} Osmani identifies scheduling, isolated worktrees, reusable skills, connectors and speci","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">\noindent\textbf{What turns repeated prompting into an engineered loop?} Osmani identifies scheduling, isolated worktrees, reusable skills, connectors and speci</data>
      <data key="k263">88</data>
      <data key="k265">88</data>
      <data key="k306">\noindent\textbf{What turns repeated prompting into an engineered loop?} Osmani identifies scheduling, isolated worktrees, reusable skills, connectors and specialist subagents as common operational building blocks, and a durable external task or memory record as an additional continuity mechanism \osmanicite{O012}. His practical-loop discussion adds the need for a defined finish and risk-aware oversight \osmanicite{O006}. These mechanisms fit the state machine above only when the surrounding system assigns each a controlled responsibility:</data>
      <data key="k446">f05b77b17befaa0c91e9069613ffc1ee3488308661ed779ef62a1ed83027eda7</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter03.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["O006", "O012"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0036">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0036","kind":"cited_passage","label":"\\noindent\\textbf{Keep the controls; question the extra machinery.} Orosz's public discussion of loop engineering asks whether custom coordination still earns it","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">\noindent\textbf{Keep the controls; question the extra machinery.} Orosz's public discussion of loop engineering asks whether custom coordination still earns it</data>
      <data key="k263">110</data>
      <data key="k265">110</data>
      <data key="k306">\noindent\textbf{Keep the controls; question the extra machinery.} Orosz's public discussion of loop engineering asks whether custom coordination still earns its complexity when existing tooling can supply some of the same mechanisms \oroszcite{P110}. This is a useful challenge to implementation choices, not evidence that the engineering responsibilities disappear. A human-led assistant, a bounded agent execution environment (a harness) and a custom coordinator can distribute those responsibilities differently. None is adequate merely because its name suggests autonomy or safety.</data>
      <data key="k446">83fe899a1c899f472c717b434a9f7f3071670664ecc16a9f42aeeb1eaca720b2</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter03.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["P110"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0037">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0037","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Hassan\\textquotesingle s research roadmap \\autocite{W152} discusses the architectural questions surrounding agentic software engineering, while Kim\\textquotesin","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Hassan\textquotesingle s research roadmap \autocite{W152} discusses the architectural questions surrounding agentic software engineering, while Kim\textquotesin</data>
      <data key="k263">131</data>
      <data key="k265">131</data>
      <data key="k306">Hassan\textquotesingle s research roadmap \autocite{W152} discusses the architectural questions surrounding agentic software engineering, while Kim\textquotesingle s work on correlated model errors \autocite{W079} motivates caution about apparent consensus. These sources do not prove a general throughput gain from multi-agent development. The gain depends on decomposition, integration cost and the independence of verification.</data>
      <data key="k446">c5e35852ea071121e67256c603944fe2f206b999f86947cb5604ba41d3a30e9d</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter03.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W079", "W152"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0038">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0038","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Whitfill\\textquotesingle s study of benchmark-passing yet unmergeable patches \\autocite{W111} motivates the distinction between local pass and maintainer accept","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Whitfill\textquotesingle s study of benchmark-passing yet unmergeable patches \autocite{W111} motivates the distinction between local pass and maintainer accept</data>
      <data key="k263">139</data>
      <data key="k265">139</data>
      <data key="k306">Whitfill\textquotesingle s study of benchmark-passing yet unmergeable patches \autocite{W111} motivates the distinction between local pass and maintainer acceptance. Purnachandra Reddy\textquotesingle s work on self-review failures in code modernization \autocite{W101} raises a related concern about trusting a model\textquotesingle s own explanation. The exact empirical rates remain outside this draft until the papers\textquotesingle{} result tables are verified. The governance rule we propose is operational: a checker or orchestrator must cite a concrete failing oracle, missing evidence or named policy before rejecting an otherwise green patch. After two repeated rejections without a traceable hook, autonomous retries stop and the case escalates to a named human.</data>
      <data key="k446">dc54a03614d86e2c5d46ccb285bc9f0cc42dd6609341aaee286d98b84b8743da</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter03.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W101", "W111"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0039">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0039","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Bhati\\textquotesingle s lifecycle discussion \\autocite{W095} and Farrag\\textquotesingle s specification-driven governance proposal \\autocite{W011} offer concept","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Bhati\textquotesingle s lifecycle discussion \autocite{W095} and Farrag\textquotesingle s specification-driven governance proposal \autocite{W011} offer concept</data>
      <data key="k263">159</data>
      <data key="k265">159</data>
      <data key="k306">Bhati\textquotesingle s lifecycle discussion \autocite{W095} and Farrag\textquotesingle s specification-driven governance proposal \autocite{W011} offer conceptual ways to connect generation with reliability and cost. They should be read as proposed frameworks rather than controlled validation of the intent-first method used in this book. Lobentanzer\textquotesingle s expectation--realisation discussion \autocite{W013} supplies a contrasting warning about promises that outrun measured outcomes.</data>
      <data key="k446">b5a20350f0e66a94dd4fef4e82d107bd16a83cf8bf084d016f895aaf0af2e908</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter03.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W011", "W013", "W095"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0040">
