    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0044">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0044","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Farrag\\textquotesingle s specification-governance discussion \\autocite{W011} and Hoda\\textquotesingle s research on software work \\autocite{W153} motivate disti","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Farrag\textquotesingle s specification-governance discussion \autocite{W011} and Hoda\textquotesingle s research on software work \autocite{W153} motivate disti</data>
      <data key="k263">21</data>
      <data key="k265">21</data>
      <data key="k306">Farrag\textquotesingle s specification-governance discussion \autocite{W011} and Hoda\textquotesingle s research on software work \autocite{W153} motivate distinguishing problem framing from implementation activity; Ehsani\textquotesingle s failed-agent PR study \autocite{W191} offers a relevant counterpoint about unsuccessful candidate changes. Their presence in the knowledge graph does not experimentally validate our phase separation. The proposed rule is narrower: authorize the external outcome at I1, then allow alternative designs at D0. Questions discovered later return to the intention authority instead of being answered by a model\textquotesingle s confident guess.</data>
      <data key="k446">f6b25ee8294095051b73548ad0ebcb3b211d1cd83324102a91e5c967f7a04998</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter04.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W011", "W153", "W191"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0045">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0045","kind":"cited_passage","label":"\\noindent\\textbf{Who owns a specification?} Osmani's advice on writing specifications for coding agents \\osmanicite{O018} is useful for decomposition, explicit ","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">\noindent\textbf{Who owns a specification?} Osmani's advice on writing specifications for coding agents \osmanicite{O018} is useful for decomposition, explicit </data>
      <data key="k263">29</data>
      <data key="k265">29</data>
      <data key="k306">\noindent\textbf{Who owns a specification?} Osmani's advice on writing specifications for coding agents \osmanicite{O018} is useful for decomposition, explicit constraints and testable expectations. The warning is in the word \emph{truth}: a generated \texttt{SPEC.md} is a working proposal, not a policy decision. His separation of exploratory vibe coding and disciplined engineering similarly turns on which artifacts are reviewed and owned \osmanicite{O028}. In our running case three records have different authorship and authority:</data>
      <data key="k446">415e2adebd59f3c56a703d0ca942d46d553d09a21b4ae57ae752a915448df39f</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter04.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["O018", "O028"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0046">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0046","kind":"cited_passage","label":"The ledger should also record uncertainty. Suppose the existing service rounds monetary values before comparing thresholds. Is rounding a contractual rule or an","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">The ledger should also record uncertainty. Suppose the existing service rounds monetary values before comparing thresholds. Is rounding a contractual rule or an</data>
      <data key="k263">57</data>
      <data key="k265">57</data>
      <data key="k306">The ledger should also record uncertainty. Suppose the existing service rounds monetary values before comparing thresholds. Is rounding a contractual rule or an implementation defect? The ledger marks it unresolved and assigns a decision owner. Chen\textquotesingle s and Hora\textquotesingle s work on team and organizational coordination \autocite{W150}, together with Hassan\textquotesingle s agentic engineering roadmap \autocite{W152}, provides useful context for authority and handoffs, but the particular JSON schema remains our proposal. The practical benefit of the ledger is that later reviewers can tell what was approved, what was assumed and what changed.</data>
      <data key="k446">93542c4358555042e51b2a6799a2351a308a8942962c7d71e7b8db1888ebb6f4</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter04.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W150", "W152"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0047">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0047","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Drew Hoskins's discussion of diagnostics, excerpted and introduced in Orosz's publication, treats useful errors and warnings as part of the product experience \\","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Drew Hoskins's discussion of diagnostics, excerpted and introduced in Orosz's publication, treats useful errors and warnings as part of the product experience \</data>
      <data key="k263">78</data>
      <data key="k265">78</data>
      <data key="k306">Drew Hoskins's discussion of diagnostics, excerpted and introduced in Orosz's publication, treats useful errors and warnings as part of the product experience \oroszcite{P082}. Apply that idea to the acceptance matrix: specify the recipient, stable condition, relevant policy revision, permitted next step and forbidden side effects of each response. A diagnostic describes the situation; it does not authorize a policy change.</data>
      <data key="k446">2a8f0dc169ece46c906f8cb154d7b03660aea47046201e2bb71bbb3a1e011614</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter04.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["P082"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0048">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0048","kind":"cited_passage","label":"The examples should not be generated solely by reading the implementation. Doing so risks turning a bug into an acceptance criterion. They must come from an app","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">The examples should not be generated solely by reading the implementation. Doing so risks turning a bug into an acceptance criterion. They must come from an app</data>
      <data key="k263">87</data>
      <data key="k265">87</data>
      <data key="k306">The examples should not be generated solely by reading the implementation. Doing so risks turning a bug into an acceptance criterion. They must come from an approved policy statement, with developers and test designers free to challenge missing boundary cases. Zhao\textquotesingle s verification research \autocite{W077} and related work on test quality \autocite{W097} motivate the importance of explicit external oracles, but the exact case matrix is author-created and synthetic.</data>
      <data key="k446">481ad5db8d14baa7a45d3c9d7befda0d226c2c85f51d7229740016b6334df2e5</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter04.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W077", "W097"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0049">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0049","kind":"cited_passage","label":"A generated test can preserve the very defect it is meant to expose when its expected answer is inferred from the implementation. Zhao, Zhou and Cohen examine t","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">A generated test can preserve the very defect it is meant to expose when its expected answer is inferred from the implementation. Zhao, Zhou and Cohen examine t</data>
      <data key="k263">101</data>
      <data key="k265">101</data>
      <data key="k306">A generated test can preserve the very defect it is meant to expose when its expected answer is inferred from the implementation. Zhao, Zhou and Cohen examine that failure mode using buggy and fixed program versions. Chapter~\ref{chap:07} develops the independent-oracle response and the study\textquotesingle s design limits. Here the implication is a contract rule: fix the intended behavior before using implementation output as test evidence. \autocite{W077}</data>
      <data key="k446">494cbae2d6fb42cdc0ebc1e50460477d17f2c48e55c6d0293164fd2ca1493846</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter04.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W077"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0050">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0050","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Kaufman and colleagues provide a complementary human-review warning: in their experiment with 86 Python programmers, participants identified correct generated a","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Kaufman and colleagues provide a complementary human-review warning: in their experiment with 86 Python programmers, participants identified correct generated a</data>
      <data key="k263">103</data>
      <data key="k265">103</data>
      <data key="k306">Kaufman and colleagues provide a complementary human-review warning: in their experiment with 86 Python programmers, participants identified correct generated assertions with 74\% accuracy but incorrect assertions with only 49\% accuracy, while reporting similar confidence. This is evidence about judgment of assertions in that study, not a universal human-review failure rate. Together the papers motivate separating contract authorship, implementation and verification, then reviewing the test oracle itself. \autocite{W097}</data>
      <data key="k446">491b9579f183eb9c296a7bcc49e9382bc42b33dcf7e3e70f4b6773df5e6db45f</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter04.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W097"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0051">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0051","kind":"cited_passage","label":"The knowledge graph connects code-review and architecture research (\\autocite{W099}, \\autocite{W152}) with the engineering-structure literature \\autocite{W148}.","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">The knowledge graph connects code-review and architecture research (\autocite{W099}, \autocite{W152}) with the engineering-structure literature \autocite{W148}.</data>
      <data key="k263">124</data>
      <data key="k265">124</data>
      <data key="k306">The knowledge graph connects code-review and architecture research (\autocite{W099}, \autocite{W152}) with the engineering-structure literature \autocite{W148}. These sources inform what to examine---diff scope, architectural drift and process assumptions---but do not authorize a design choice for this fictional service. If a proposed design reveals that the contract cannot be satisfied, the team raises a decision request at I1 or I2. It does not redefine success by making tests match the easiest implementation.</data>
      <data key="k446">de12fe3d71499b4295728b1c59723861214cc1122a7fab12cff0792dbebab5f0</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter04.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W099", "W148", "W152"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0052">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0052","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Hassan\\textquotesingle s roadmap \\autocite{W152} and research on rejected agent PRs \\autocite{W071} motivate coordination and review without establishing an opt","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Hassan\textquotesingle s roadmap \autocite{W152} and research on rejected agent PRs \autocite{W071} motivate coordination and review without establishing an opt</data>
      <data key="k263">135</data>
      <data key="k265">135</data>
      <data key="k306">Hassan\textquotesingle s roadmap \autocite{W152} and research on rejected agent PRs \autocite{W071} motivate coordination and review without establishing an optimal gate sequence. Figure~\ref{fig:ch04-research-2} makes our proposed sequence explicit: validate the authorized delta before asking the model for a new patch, then rerun the affected contracts and independent checks. The dashed return brings unresolved evidence back to the control layer; it does not let the model silently rewrite intention. This keeps a late requirement small enough to trace without regenerating the entire system.</data>
      <data key="k446">31d5a95b7efee7b1f7427ad193ba84c1073f94aaab7d7af9d27f50367edf6f59</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter04.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W071", "W152"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0053">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0053","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Qiu\\textquotesingle s blueprint-first workflow \\autocite{W151} provides an architectural analogy for constraining model execution, while Farrag\\textquotesingle ","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Qiu\textquotesingle s blueprint-first workflow \autocite{W151} provides an architectural analogy for constraining model execution, while Farrag\textquotesingle </data>
      <data key="k263">163</data>
      <data key="k265">163</data>
      <data key="k306">Qiu\textquotesingle s blueprint-first workflow \autocite{W151} provides an architectural analogy for constraining model execution, while Farrag\textquotesingle s specification governance \autocite{W011} motivates explicit checks. Neither source validates the exact intent-first state machine. The gate definitions are a normative engineering proposal, with observable exit conditions and named decision authority. The objective is to make the process inspectable while keeping change control lightweight enough for repeated increments.</data>
      <data key="k446">65c61cb89a64d3a3464657591c60a090da5791cc999d0caa8888d11ecbf3a90d</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter04.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W011", "W151"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0054">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0054","kind":"cited_passage","label":"\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,height=.62\\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/jpg/10_Figure_4_1_Architect.jpg}\n\\caption{Deterministic control surrounds model","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=.62\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/jpg/10_Figure_4_1_Architect.jpg}
\caption{Deterministic control surrounds model</data>
      <data key="k263">33</data>
      <data key="k265">29</data>
      <data key="k306">\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=.62\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/jpg/10_Figure_4_1_Architect.jpg}
\caption{Deterministic control surrounds model-generated substeps. Conceptual redraw of W151, original Figure 2; \workref{W151}. Based on the Source Code Agent design; its original evaluation was travel planning, not brownfield change approval. Change approval and independent acceptance remain external to model output.}
\label{fig:ch04-research-1}
\end{figure}
</data>
      <data key="k446">1b3ec6dbd2e9d4aba12a4ffbd103ac5bb8417874ed4fc2e779869473a7720e93</data>
