    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0071">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0071","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Kapner et al.~\\autocite{W053} study 3,171 public repositories: 2,660 assembled coding-agent setups and 511 skill collections. Their original PDF reports unpinne","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Kapner et al.~\autocite{W053} study 3,171 public repositories: 2,660 assembled coding-agent setups and 511 skill collections. Their original PDF reports unpinne</data>
      <data key="k263">18</data>
      <data key="k265">18</data>
      <data key="k306">Kapner et al.~\autocite{W053} study 3,171 public repositories: 2,660 assembled coding-agent setups and 511 skill collections. Their original PDF reports unpinned MCP declarations in 9.8\% of setups, broad execution grants in 2.5\% and broad skill-tool preapproval in 3.8\%. The union of the three security-exposure categories affects 409 setups (15.4\%). These are configuration findings, not observed successful compromises. The authors explicitly state that runtime consequences and scanner recall remain unmeasured (\autocite{W053}, PDF pp.~1, 3--4, abstract, Table 1, §4). \autocite{W053}</data>
      <data key="k446">a025dbf5311bd902b24c42f2f642eaa6235839a142e7a8bdca2f1cc39bcaa157</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter06.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W053"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0072">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0072","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Liu et al.\\textquotesingle s Your AI, My Shell \\autocite{W060} addresses runtime hijacking through poisoned development resources. Its original abstract describ","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Liu et al.\textquotesingle s Your AI, My Shell \autocite{W060} addresses runtime hijacking through poisoned development resources. Its original abstract describ</data>
      <data key="k263">20</data>
      <data key="k265">20</data>
      <data key="k306">Liu et al.\textquotesingle s Your AI, My Shell \autocite{W060} addresses runtime hijacking through poisoned development resources. Its original abstract describes 314 attack payloads covering 70 MITRE ATT\&amp;CK techniques, with success reaching 84\% in studied editor configurations. This is a study-specific maximum, not a general attack probability; the large PDF remains pending page-level inspection. \autocite{W060}</data>
      <data key="k446">98d93a7c6e82abbfe6c6025b453f815820dcef833ad409117cad03ebb5513e24</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter06.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W060"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0073">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0073","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Zhao et al.\\textquotesingle s SUSVIBES study \\autocite{W045} contains 186 security-sensitive feature tasks mined from real vulnerability-fix histories, covering","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Zhao et al.\textquotesingle s SUSVIBES study \autocite{W045} contains 186 security-sensitive feature tasks mined from real vulnerability-fix histories, covering</data>
      <data key="k263">42</data>
      <data key="k265">42</data>
      <data key="k306">Zhao et al.\textquotesingle s SUSVIBES study \autocite{W045} contains 186 security-sensitive feature tasks mined from real vulnerability-fix histories, covering 79 CWE categories. It evaluates functional and security tests separately. In the reported SWE-Agent/Claude 4 Sonnet setting, approximately 57\% of solutions pass functional tests, but only 11.8\% satisfy the security criterion; the authors report that 79.3\% of functionally correct solutions fail security checks. These percentages describe the selected benchmark and agent setting, not production incident rates (\autocite{W045}, PDF pp.~1--2, abstract and introduction; pp.~4--5, task verification). \autocite{W045}</data>
      <data key="k446">0e8a6755650d91a3bbb2563cc8f7bedd474130db154a182a3706fe1a59c3616f</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter06.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W045"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0074">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0074","kind":"cited_passage","label":"\\autocite{W041} offers a particularly important denominator caution. The authors\\textquotesingle{} initial code-search results include documentation and other n","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">\autocite{W041} offers a particularly important denominator caution. The authors\textquotesingle{} initial code-search results include documentation and other n</data>
      <data key="k263">48</data>
      <data key="k265">48</data>
      <data key="k306">\autocite{W041} offers a particularly important denominator caution. The authors\textquotesingle{} initial code-search results include documentation and other non-executable artifacts, and the later CodeQL sample focuses on selected languages. Even within the filtered sample, a file with no detected CWE finding is not thereby proven secure. Scanner coverage, query configuration and unsupported vulnerability classes all affect what can be found. Comparing this result with \workposs{W045} security-test pass rate would mix a file-level static-analysis endpoint with a task-level dynamic benchmark.</data>
      <data key="k446">cfd61db80e31707acd4a58be4380e7fb9afcefc3245786272f5da849dbf737d8</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter06.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W041", "W045"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0075">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0075","kind":"cited_passage","label":"The strongest interpretation is therefore conditional: in \\workposs{W041} explicitly attributed and filtered files, the selected scanner detected particular CWE","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">The strongest interpretation is therefore conditional: in \workposs{W041} explicitly attributed and filtered files, the selected scanner detected particular CWE</data>
      <data key="k263">50</data>
      <data key="k265">50</data>
      <data key="k306">The strongest interpretation is therefore conditional: in \workposs{W041} explicitly attributed and filtered files, the selected scanner detected particular CWE-mapped patterns; in \workposs{W045} selected feature tasks, agents frequently satisfied functionality without passing the security oracle. Both findings motivate independent checks, but neither supplies a universal vulnerability percentage for every AI-assisted commit.</data>
      <data key="k446">6e502421efb90e3f153c401a96171a81ae253467a48851d55915e4aa8db305dc</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter06.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W041", "W045"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0076">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0076","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Perry et al.~\\autocite{W046} instead conduct a human-user experiment. Participants with AI assistance wrote less secure code on the studied tasks and expressed ","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Perry et al.~\autocite{W046} instead conduct a human-user experiment. Participants with AI assistance wrote less secure code on the studied tasks and expressed </data>
      <data key="k263">54</data>
      <data key="k265">54</data>
      <data key="k306">Perry et al.~\autocite{W046} instead conduct a human-user experiment. Participants with AI assistance wrote less secure code on the studied tasks and expressed greater confidence in their solutions. The result concerns a specific set of people, tasks and early tools; it is not directly comparable to the \autocite{W041} scanner counts (\autocite{W046}, PDF p.~1, abstract; detailed experiment requires version-specific checking). \autocite{W046}</data>
      <data key="k446">8a248c78fc29aa7ffe02a09d24820cb77dff65c4db6660b14288bfbcb0600b7c</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter06.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W041", "W046"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0077">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0077","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Yan et al.~\\autocite{W038} investigate security hints and explanation-guided repair in Python benchmarks. Their paper discusses an archive-extraction case in wh","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Yan et al.~\autocite{W038} investigate security hints and explanation-guided repair in Python benchmarks. Their paper discusses an archive-extraction case in wh</data>
      <data key="k263">66</data>
      <data key="k265">66</data>
      <data key="k306">Yan et al.~\autocite{W038} investigate security hints and explanation-guided repair in Python benchmarks. Their paper discusses an archive-extraction case in which explaining the path-traversal mechanism guides a containment check for every archive member. The authors also acknowledge that benefits diminish when repairs are distributed or configuration-heavy, and that CodeQL cannot detect every dynamic vulnerability (\autocite{W038}, PDF pp.~26--28, case study and threats to validity). \autocite{W038}</data>
      <data key="k446">6c58f2224efef2183cef460af67b378e525371b6faf9730207a203d60be66e54</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter06.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W038"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0078">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0078","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Spracklen et al.~\\autocite{W043} study package hallucinations across 16 models and 576,000 generated code samples in Python and JavaScript. Their original PDF r","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Spracklen et al.~\autocite{W043} study package hallucinations across 16 models and 576,000 generated code samples in Python and JavaScript. Their original PDF r</data>
      <data key="k263">82</data>
      <data key="k265">82</data>
      <data key="k306">Spracklen et al.~\autocite{W043} study package hallucinations across 16 models and 576,000 generated code samples in Python and JavaScript. Their original PDF reports substantial differences across models and settings. These are generated package recommendations, not observations that every hallucinated name was registered or installed by an attacker (\autocite{W043}, PDF p.~1, abstract and subsequent methods). \autocite{W043}</data>
      <data key="k446">f6b0f6fe4c09d7f01b37e310d72f03aa3922a690abaf85232883b994e9b8b7b9</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter06.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W043"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0079">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0079","kind":"cited_passage","label":"\\autocite{W060} shows the relevance of this problem for editors with shell and filesystem access. \\autocite{W030} investigates workspace topology as an attack v","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">\autocite{W060} shows the relevance of this problem for editors with shell and filesystem access. \autocite{W030} investigates workspace topology as an attack v</data>
      <data key="k263">98</data>
      <data key="k265">98</data>
      <data key="k306">\autocite{W060} shows the relevance of this problem for editors with shell and filesystem access. \autocite{W030} investigates workspace topology as an attack vector, while \autocite{W104} studies human detection of agent sabotage. The latter two papers remain pending full-text inspection, so no numerical effect is attributed to them here.</data>
      <data key="k446">259e5955220da72121c1fe869d04e5343b8956afef39dd3ed180979aa31776dd</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter06.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W030", "W060", "W104"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0080">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0080","kind":"cited_passage","label":"The \\autocite{W053} authors\\textquotesingle{} own scanner correction is a useful warning about automated policy checks. A rule may be syntactically precise yet ","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">The \autocite{W053} authors\textquotesingle{} own scanner correction is a useful warning about automated policy checks. A rule may be syntactically precise yet </data>
      <data key="k263">106</data>
      <data key="k265">106</data>
      <data key="k306">The \autocite{W053} authors\textquotesingle{} own scanner correction is a useful warning about automated policy checks. A rule may be syntactically precise yet misunderstand the platform\textquotesingle s permission semantics. Before deploying a harness scanner as a hard gate, test it on allowed and denied examples, include known exceptions and record the scanner version. A green scanner report should mean that the configured checks passed, not that every possible configuration risk was excluded.</data>
      <data key="k446">bf37bd636f0cac8e9d1598202d7a2f6afb45552338a6cef48374dc5444995d0a</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter06.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W053"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0081">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0081","kind":"cited_passage","label":"\\autocite{W053} reports that 24.5\\% of setups containing MCP configuration have an unpinned package declaration. The paper also describes a semantic mistake sha","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">\autocite{W053} reports that 24.5\% of setups containing MCP configuration have an unpinned package declaration. The paper also describes a semantic mistake sha</data>
      <data key="k263">110</data>
      <data key="k265">110</data>
      <data key="k306">\autocite{W053} reports that 24.5\% of setups containing MCP configuration have an unpinned package declaration. The paper also describes a semantic mistake shared by the original scanner and its mechanical audit, emphasizing that configuration checks must be tested against the platform\textquotesingle s actual permission semantics (\autocite{W053}, PDF pp.~3--4, §4). These observations motivate reviewing harness declarations but do not measure how often the identified setups are exploited.</data>
      <data key="k446">031494516ff97787adef35edbf8667abb256d315b44ec59338caba53f5a6cbbc</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter06.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W053"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0082">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0082","kind":"cited_passage","label":"\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,height=.62\\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/jpg/15_Figure_6_1_Architect.jpg}\n\\caption{The package-hallucination supply-chai","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=.62\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/jpg/15_Figure_6_1_Architect.jpg}
\caption{The package-hallucination supply-chai</data>
      <data key="k263">32</data>
      <data key="k265">28</data>
      <data key="k306">\includegraphics[width=\linewidth,height=.62\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/jpg/15_Figure_6_1_Architect.jpg}
\caption{The package-hallucination supply-chain path. Conceptual redraw of W043, original Figure 1; \workref{W043}. Threat pathway from the paper; deliberately omits exploit payloads and attack instructions.}
\label{fig:ch06-research-1}
\end{figure}
</data>
