    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0127">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0127","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Shen and Tamkin's main randomized study enrolled 52 Python-experienced participants unfamiliar with Trio, 26 per condition. After two tasks and up to 35 minutes","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Shen and Tamkin's main randomized study enrolled 52 Python-experienced participants unfamiliar with Trio, 26 per condition. After two tasks and up to 35 minutes</data>
      <data key="k263">15</data>
      <data key="k265">15</data>
      <data key="k306">Shen and Tamkin's main randomized study enrolled 52 Python-experienced participants unfamiliar with Trio, 26 per condition. After two tasks and up to 35 minutes of work, participants completed an unaided 14-question assessment. The authors report lower immediate comprehension with AI assistance (Cohen's $d=0.738$, $p=0.010$), without a statistically significant average improvement in task time. This is an immediate assessment, not a week- or month-later retention result. The source's percentage summary has an unresolved normalization inconsistency, recorded in Appendix~\ref{app:methodology}, \S\ref{app:commentary-ch10}; the chapter does not turn it into a precise loss-of-skill rate. \autocite{W118}</data>
      <data key="k446">82b05b17064d48848cb5f18c6a3e3f8367b1ab24c43f8c4ed626dbb999e25116</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter10.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W118"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0128">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0128","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Ahmad's qualitative study analyzed 621 reflective diaries from 207 students over eight weeks. It describes black-box acceptance, context mismatch, dependency-in","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Ahmad's qualitative study analyzed 621 reflective diaries from 207 students over eight weeks. It describes black-box acceptance, context mismatch, dependency-in</data>
      <data key="k263">28</data>
      <data key="k265">28</data>
      <data key="k306">Ahmad's qualitative study analyzed 621 reflective diaries from 207 students over eight weeks. It describes black-box acceptance, context mismatch, dependency-induced atrophy and verification bypass, alongside a contrasting use of AI as a comprehension scaffold. These are recurring experiences in an undergraduate project, not an eight-week controlled retention test or measured industrial defect rates. \autocite{W154}</data>
      <data key="k446">3c6c5cf7271468d5232081c61b570f85b3f043034017ff998b82b821ae71cfd5</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter10.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W154"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0129">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0129","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Anderson and colleagues examine changes to unfamiliar code using architecture sketches and explanations as well as task completion. That small study motivates a","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Anderson and colleagues examine changes to unfamiliar code using architecture sketches and explanations as well as task completion. That small study motivates a</data>
      <data key="k263">30</data>
      <data key="k265">30</data>
      <data key="k306">Anderson and colleagues examine changes to unfamiliar code using architecture sketches and explanations as well as task completion. That small study motivates assessing the mental model behind a patch; it does not establish its future maintenance consequences. The source's method and verification boundaries remain in Appendix~B. \autocite{W155}</data>
      <data key="k446">0904e392b12311e8becf75cae3c4b2ceadf0991857091d38e383a83c3cd6a47a</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter10.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W155"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0130">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0130","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Zi and colleagues studied 32 introductory-programming students across 160 task instances. The reported 32.5\\% success rate concerns their generated-code \\emph{c","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Zi and colleagues studied 32 introductory-programming students across 160 task instances. The reported 32.5\% success rate concerns their generated-code \emph{c</data>
      <data key="k263">44</data>
      <data key="k265">44</data>
      <data key="k306">Zi and colleagues studied 32 introductory-programming students across 160 task instances. The reported 32.5\% success rate concerns their generated-code \emph{comprehension task}; it is not a defect rate in generated programs. The authors describe difficulties involving Python familiarity, language comprehension and automation bias. The population and tasks limit transfer to experienced maintainers. \autocite{W113}</data>
      <data key="k446">2e0bc5b8b0a5a6c6eb662318ff4e3936e231189f6ad2adbf0b982ba340b06820</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter10.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W113"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0131">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0131","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Related work on novice contributions and attention to security motivates reviewing generated assertions before delegating cross-module changes; their uncomplete","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Related work on novice contributions and attention to security motivates reviewing generated assertions before delegating cross-module changes; their uncomplete</data>
      <data key="k263">48</data>
      <data key="k265">48</data>
      <data key="k306">Related work on novice contributions and attention to security motivates reviewing generated assertions before delegating cross-module changes; their uncompleted primary-method audits do not justify numerical learning claims here. \autocite{W112,W067} Increase delegated responsibility with demonstrated judgment rather than job title or prompt count.</data>
      <data key="k446">0607372020acfa8cb578318797c478d3d1a5192e87e573f76bcc22db58bcf3af</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter10.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W067", "W112"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0132">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0132","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Lower perceived effort, eye-fixation measures or physiological signals are not interchangeable with later unaided performance. The biometric and offloading sour","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Lower perceived effort, eye-fixation measures or physiological signals are not interchangeable with later unaided performance. The biometric and offloading sour</data>
      <data key="k263">55</data>
      <data key="k265">55</data>
      <data key="k306">Lower perceived effort, eye-fixation measures or physiological signals are not interchangeable with later unaided performance. The biometric and offloading sources in the catalogue do not yet support a fully audited physiological effect here. \autocite{W116,W117} Work on essay writing is an adjacent comparison, not direct evidence of coding-skill loss. \autocite{W121}</data>
      <data key="k446">bb215d8fd99ae91672d74d73a91908628e366635a49ebf24520d371385d8173b</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter10.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W116", "W117", "W121"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0133">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0133","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Kara and colleagues' LACY system combines expert-curated tours with AI-generated explanations. Its industry co-design involved a legacy finance system exceeding","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Kara and colleagues' LACY system combines expert-curated tours with AI-generated explanations. Its industry co-design involved a legacy finance system exceeding</data>
      <data key="k263">66</data>
      <data key="k265">66</data>
      <data key="k306">Kara and colleagues' LACY system combines expert-curated tours with AI-generated explanations. Its industry co-design involved a legacy finance system exceeding 30,000 lines; reported quiz scores were 83\% for expert-guided tours and 57\% for AI-only tours. This is an immediate study outcome, not demonstrated retention months later. The intervention combines generation, curation and a reusable code-anchored narrative, rather than merely comparing the presence or absence of AI. \autocite{W156}</data>
      <data key="k446">6e7f5be290371970ebcb52d82928783b725d5f0b85aa401cb9dee0fc20764582</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter10.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W156"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0134">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0134","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Onboarding also has social and procedural dimensions. Ju and colleagues' Microsoft study combines interviews with newly assigned developers and managers with fo","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Onboarding also has social and procedural dimensions. Ju and colleagues' Microsoft study combines interviews with newly assigned developers and managers with fo</data>
      <data key="k263">70</data>
      <data key="k265">70</data>
      <data key="k306">Onboarding also has social and procedural dimensions. Ju and colleagues' Microsoft study combines interviews with newly assigned developers and managers with follow-up surveys. Its exploratory mixed-method design motivates attention to ownership and access to help; it does not identify an AI-mentoring treatment effect. \autocite{W163}</data>
      <data key="k446">dca79a53b2a7f94f4bb2e0f4ee8ea35e31d6cbff9bb86cb9e112ef06fb19703d</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter10.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W163"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0135">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0135","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Historical work on developer fluency, ramp-up and distributed development motivates a staged view, not a universal time-to-productivity curve. Its remaining pri","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Historical work on developer fluency, ramp-up and distributed development motivates a staged view, not a universal time-to-productivity curve. Its remaining pri</data>
      <data key="k263">81</data>
      <data key="k265">81</data>
      <data key="k306">Historical work on developer fluency, ramp-up and distributed development motivates a staged view, not a universal time-to-productivity curve. Its remaining primary-source checks are recorded in Appendix~B. \autocite{W184,W178,W187} A local progression moves from explaining current behavior to repairing a bounded defect, reviewing another candidate, introducing a versioned rule and diagnosing an unseen failure. Each step provides a different opportunity to observe independent judgment.</data>
      <data key="k446">b77cc4af77e5e6b8ccda0635e0a4d467c822e4ef4c029688a969ad80634b9d62</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter10.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W178", "W184", "W187"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0136">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0136","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Routine tasks can be both production work and occasions for supervised practice. Removing them changes the opportunities through which novices become independen","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Routine tasks can be both production work and occasions for supervised practice. Removing them changes the opportunities through which novices become independen</data>
      <data key="k263">88</data>
      <data key="k265">88</data>
      <data key="k306">Routine tasks can be both production work and occasions for supervised practice. Removing them changes the opportunities through which novices become independent, even when immediate output improves. The apprenticeship model offers a theoretical way to frame that trade-off; Chapter~\ref{chap:12} examines its economic assumptions. It does not establish a staffing rule or forecast this team\textquotesingle s skill supply. \autocite{W119}</data>
      <data key="k446">ca0a7cf98fb663d2481f9c18bff8694efca0c268e820b8f648d0a3535a3df146</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter10.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W119"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0137">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0137","kind":"cited_passage","label":"\\autocite{W162} maps the designs of 95 code-comprehension experiments published from 1979 to 2019: the corpus predates present-day agentic coding and is a syste","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">\autocite{W162} maps the designs of 95 code-comprehension experiments published from 1979 to 2019: the corpus predates present-day agentic coding and is a syste</data>
      <data key="k263">109</data>
      <data key="k265">109</data>
      <data key="k306">\autocite{W162} maps the designs of 95 code-comprehension experiments published from 1979 to 2019: the corpus predates present-day agentic coding and is a systematic mapping, not a controlled benchmark of coding agents. Its ACM Computing Surveys version of record is volume~56, issue~4 (2024), article~106. A \emph{September 2026 corrigendum} \autocite{W162Corrigendum2026} documents a misordering that caused primary-study identifiers S1--S95 in the article and tables to disagree with the study reference list and replication package; a reanalysis must use the corrected mapping. \autocite{W170} observes professional activity but is not an intervention study. \autocite{W114} explores physiological correlates; its original full text still needs inspection, and those signals cannot substitute for demonstrated reasoning.</data>
      <data key="k446">af8a2b5aeb711f15f890feb0147b26272f81961bf15a74117c5a114b96c2c1ab</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter10.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W114", "W162", "W162Corrigendum2026", "W170"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIB:W162Corrigendum2026">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":["C:C10"],"authors":"","category":"provenance","collection":null,"historical_v12_actual":[],"historical_v12_mapped":[],"historical_v12_printed":false,"id":"BIB:W162Corrigendum2026","kind":"auxiliary_bibliography","label":"Corrigendum: 40 Years of Designing Code Comprehension Experiments: A Systematic Mapping Study","limitation":"","mapped":["C:C10"],"printed":true,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3838282","volumes":[],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k162">AUX</data>
