    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0142">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0142","kind":"cited_passage","label":"A field measurement should distinguish the arrival process from service capacity and downstream outcomes. The queueing example is deliberately deterministic, bu","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">A field measurement should distinguish the arrival process from service capacity and downstream outcomes. The queueing example is deliberately deterministic, bu</data>
      <data key="k263">15</data>
      <data key="k265">15</data>
      <data key="k306">A field measurement should distinguish the arrival process from service capacity and downstream outcomes. The queueing example is deliberately deterministic, but a real team must also record task mix and variance: policy-sensitive changes may take longer to review than documentation. It should retain all candidate attempts, including those withdrawn before review, so an increase in generation cannot masquerade as an improvement in acceptance. Afroz et al.~\autocite{W094} report an estimated 519.86 additional pull requests per week across 294 sampled repositories relative to their Bayesian structural-time-series counterfactual, approximately 27,032 over 52 weeks. Their Table II also reports an estimated 1.06\% decline in aggregate PR merge ratio and an 18.18\% decline for one-time contributors. The intervention is a calendar-period proxy for the AI surge, not randomized assignment of an AI tool. It is a warning about plausible capacity pressure, not proof that AI caused every observed change.</data>
      <data key="k446">3973a83df89380beadaa1360ae5c2d50429acfc0cb5bd6890a8c730ac6ad469d</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W094"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0143">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0143","kind":"cited_passage","label":"The important distinction is between arrivals and accepted outcomes. Chapter~\\ref{chap:02} examines how production-stage measurements can diverge; the open-sour","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">The important distinction is between arrivals and accepted outcomes. Chapter~\ref{chap:02} examines how production-stage measurements can diverge; the open-sour</data>
      <data key="k263">21</data>
      <data key="k265">21</data>
      <data key="k306">The important distinction is between arrivals and accepted outcomes. Chapter~\ref{chap:02} examines how production-stage measurements can diverge; the open-source observations here suggest a capacity question for a team to test, rather than a private-company forecast. \autocite{W010,W094}</data>
      <data key="k446">23031e15c0dcb9ea0ace76cb5281fdfeb6724d1224524eb3d413d87f58045d5a</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W010", "W094"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0144">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0144","kind":"cited_passage","label":"The queue identifies pressure, not which work may safely disappear. Nicole Forsgren and Abi Noda's guidance, excerpted alongside Orosz's interview in \\emph{Fric","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">The queue identifies pressure, not which work may safely disappear. Nicole Forsgren and Abi Noda's guidance, excerpted alongside Orosz's interview in \emph{Fric</data>
      <data key="k263">36</data>
      <data key="k265">24</data>
      <data key="k306">The queue identifies pressure, not which work may safely disappear. Nicole Forsgren and Abi Noda's guidance, excerpted alongside Orosz's interview in \emph{Frictionless}, motivates making workflow friction visible \oroszcite{P078}. For this service, classify the purpose of a delay before optimizing it:
\begin{center}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}&gt;{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}X&gt;{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}X&gt;{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Delay and purpose} &amp; \textbf{Owner / proposed change} &amp; \textbf{Check} \\
\midrule
Repeated environment setup; avoidable preparation &amp; Platform owner versions the setup procedure. &amp; Setup effort and reproducibility; unchanged permission envelope. \\
Reviewer searches for missing provenance; assurance preparation &amp; Tech lead prepares a versioned evidence packet. &amp; Search time and missing evidence; independent checks retained. \\
Waiting for an INT-002 decision; unresolved authority &amp; Policy owner resolves the scoped question. &amp; Decision latency and traceable authorization; no inferred approval. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}</data>
      <data key="k446">00616ad60c23ec73205ddfdf57f4b819f5f7bc1ae268633ee65ce2949516f59a</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["P078"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0145">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0145","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Hassan\\textquotesingle s agentic software-engineering roadmap \\autocite{W152} identifies coordination and architecture as open research problems, while Hoda\\tex","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Hassan\textquotesingle s agentic software-engineering roadmap \autocite{W152} identifies coordination and architecture as open research problems, while Hoda\tex</data>
      <data key="k263">70</data>
      <data key="k265">70</data>
      <data key="k306">Hassan\textquotesingle s agentic software-engineering roadmap \autocite{W152} identifies coordination and architecture as open research problems, while Hoda\textquotesingle s work \autocite{W153} situates development within the social organization of software teams. These sources motivate a socio-technical account; neither proves that a seven-role chart is optimal. The proposed role model is a way to make implicit authority visible.</data>
      <data key="k446">f4b719ee98b243140ebaf9665a1bfbf4b18d3faa8301b8d82c6a4200af4e82dd</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W152", "W153"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0146">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0146","kind":"cited_passage","label":"The original policy research is more nuanced than a simple percentage of projects permitting AI. Yang et al.~\\autocite{W147} intentionally sampled 67 visible go","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">The original policy research is more nuanced than a simple percentage of projects permitting AI. Yang et al.~\autocite{W147} intentionally sampled 67 visible go</data>
      <data key="k263">78</data>
      <data key="k265">78</data>
      <data key="k306">The original policy research is more nuanced than a simple percentage of projects permitting AI. Yang et al.~\autocite{W147} intentionally sampled 67 visible governance cases---58 repositories and nine communities---using screening followed by snowball expansion. Their July 2026 arXiv v2 identifies 14 prohibitionist, 40 boundary-and-accountability, and 13 quality-first cases. The proportions are 20.9\%, 59.7\% and 19.4\% within this purposively assembled corpus, not estimates for all open-source projects. Two coders report Cohen\textquotesingle s kappa of 0.742 for dominant orientation, with macro-average kappas of 0.745 for concerns and 0.840 for strategies. These reliability statistics are author-reported and cannot be reconstructed without the coder-level labels. {[}\autocite{W147}, PDF pp.~3--5, methodology and orientation results.{]}</data>
      <data key="k446">c53de7a2b8f2d874d56601a32bc2c74ebeabb3520783f9cee41f263846422609</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W147"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0147">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0147","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Hora et al.~\\autocite{W150} studied a different unit: 281 policy documents from popular projects, plus a separate longitudinal set of 92 dedicated policy files.","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Hora et al.~\autocite{W150} studied a different unit: 281 policy documents from popular projects, plus a separate longitudinal set of 92 dedicated policy files.</data>
      <data key="k263">80</data>
      <data key="k265">80</data>
      <data key="k306">Hora et al.~\autocite{W150} studied a different unit: 281 policy documents from popular projects, plus a separate longitudinal set of 92 dedicated policy files. Their Table 1 reports 198 policies with some human-involvement guidance (70.5\%); the stricter category requiring a high level of human involvement is 67.3\%. Confusing these denominators changes the claim. Likewise, 150/281 (53.4\%) contain guidance on disclosure, but only 48.8\% make disclosure mandatory. The original manuscript labels itself submitted to ACM; it should not be cited as an accepted TOSEM article. {[}\autocite{W150}, PDF pp.~3, 7, 10--12, Tables 1--3.{]}</data>
      <data key="k446">89341db454ec2b603d7732e1abeabe4355a2226dedb41acdfac339b5b6eae4fc</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W150"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0148">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0148","kind":"cited_passage","label":"The difference between these studies matters. \\autocite{W147} describes the range of strategies projects use; \\autocite{W150} estimates how common selected prov","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">The difference between these studies matters. \autocite{W147} describes the range of strategies projects use; \autocite{W150} estimates how common selected prov</data>
      <data key="k263">87</data>
      <data key="k265">87</data>
      <data key="k306">The difference between these studies matters. \autocite{W147} describes the range of strategies projects use; \autocite{W150} estimates how common selected provisions are in its sampled policy files. Neither supplies a universal policy template or demonstrates that mandatory disclosure alone improves correctness. A written policy can change reviewer expectations without changing the quality of a generated patch.</data>
      <data key="k446">f4e0a16acd4dabc77cf832c5159238b74ceb7c18c187ba29e07c3f280378a6ab</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W147", "W150"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0149">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0149","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Qiu and colleagues\\textquotesingle{} Blueprint First, Model Second \\autocite{W151} provides an architectural analogy for keeping deterministic orchestration sep","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Qiu and colleagues\textquotesingle{} Blueprint First, Model Second \autocite{W151} provides an architectural analogy for keeping deterministic orchestration sep</data>
      <data key="k263">107</data>
      <data key="k265">107</data>
      <data key="k306">Qiu and colleagues\textquotesingle{} Blueprint First, Model Second \autocite{W151} provides an architectural analogy for keeping deterministic orchestration separate from model-generated substeps. Its evaluation is on the TravelPlanner benchmark, not enterprise brownfield engineering. Farrag \autocite{W011} proposes specification-driven governance but reports an illustrative, noncontrolled pilot. Neither paper experimentally validates this chapter\textquotesingle s gate profile.</data>
      <data key="k446">a675047cd94d2dfe34bf4b2f36d29ae37e57ad7c7c8b5021823df7e5c08259ef</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W011", "W151"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0150">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0150","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Research on agentic pull-request rejection \\autocite{W071} motivates the need to understand integration failures. FlowGen\\textquotesingle s process-model experi","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Research on agentic pull-request rejection \autocite{W071} motivates the need to understand integration failures. FlowGen\textquotesingle s process-model experi</data>
      <data key="k263">128</data>
      <data key="k265">128</data>
      <data key="k306">Research on agentic pull-request rejection \autocite{W071} motivates the need to understand integration failures. FlowGen\textquotesingle s process-model experiments \autocite{W148} concern code-generation benchmarks and should not be presented as evidence that a real team\textquotesingle s agile throughput improves under a particular agent orchestration scheme. The method must be tested against accepted changes, review time, regressions and deployment outcomes in the target environment.</data>
      <data key="k446">39bdbac25c8f63aec2d048098758dcfc21265305592be833a2b46a1a6d2e7f33</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W071", "W148"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0151">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0151","kind":"cited_passage","label":"\\noindent\\textbf{Ownership of the outer loop.} Osmani uses the ``outer loop'' to distinguish setting goals, judging evidence and living with outcomes from the i","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">\noindent\textbf{Ownership of the outer loop.} Osmani uses the ``outer loop'' to distinguish setting goals, judging evidence and living with outcomes from the i</data>
      <data key="k263">154</data>
      <data key="k265">154</data>
      <data key="k306">\noindent\textbf{Ownership of the outer loop.} Osmani uses the ``outer loop'' to distinguish setting goals, judging evidence and living with outcomes from the inner loop of agent-generated actions \osmanicite{O009}. The book's existing roles turn that phrase into a decision-rights contract. The policy owner authorizes \emph{what} the service should do and why; the orchestrator constrains permitted actions and stopping; an independent checker reports evidence against the authorized contract; a maintainer accepts the scoped code change; and the release owner controls deployment and rollback. The same person may hold multiple human roles in a small team, but no agent may inherit a missing approval merely by coordinating the other agents.</data>
      <data key="k446">18cda14cafe00f982a6590484c691b36f08c89fa20d1beaee71071cd2857a8c5</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["O009"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0152">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0152","kind":"cited_passage","label":"The strongest counterpoint to a purely restrictive policy is that governance itself can shape participation. Chen, Zimmermann and Trinkenreich \\autocite{W149} e","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">The strongest counterpoint to a purely restrictive policy is that governance itself can shape participation. Chen, Zimmermann and Trinkenreich \autocite{W149} e</data>
      <data key="k263">167</data>
      <data key="k265">167</data>
      <data key="k306">The strongest counterpoint to a purely restrictive policy is that governance itself can shape participation. Chen, Zimmermann and Trinkenreich \autocite{W149} examine 29,624 repositories and identify 385 policy adopters, using propensity-score matching and difference-in-differences. Their Table II reports changes in review activity, AI-disclosure measures and static quality proxies. But a policy requiring disclosure can alter the measurement of AI use: the authors classify AI-assisted PRs partly from observable disclosure and carry forward AI status across subsequent PRs by the same author. The treatment can therefore influence the label used for an outcome. The manuscript also contains a tension between its stated four-week post-treatment eligibility window and eight-week main-effect window. These are reasons to treat the estimates as provisional until robustness checks using direct PR-level signals and harmonized observation windows are reproduced. {[}\autocite{W149}, PDF pp.~3--4, 7--9.{]}</data>
      <data key="k446">8e5997c204eda6634924fdd58d15f42fcc14c20dca96fb06c4caca72b657fc0a</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W149"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0153">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0153","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Afroz et al.~\\autocite{W094} describe the risk of overwhelming open-source maintainers with plausible low-quality submissions. Asdaque and colleagues \\autocite{","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Afroz et al.~\autocite{W094} describe the risk of overwhelming open-source maintainers with plausible low-quality submissions. Asdaque and colleagues \autocite{</data>
      <data key="k263">171</data>
      <data key="k265">171</data>
      <data key="k306">Afroz et al.~\autocite{W094} describe the risk of overwhelming open-source maintainers with plausible low-quality submissions. Asdaque and colleagues \autocite{W112} study novice AI-assisted contributions and report substantial review overhead in their sample; their exact task controls and numerical tables require full-text verification before the book generalizes their ratios. Xu and colleagues\textquotesingle{} newcomer-participation work \autocite{W058} offers an important counterweight: code complexity and participation are not mechanically linked, and the causal design must be read on its own terms rather than reduced to a slogan.</data>
      <data key="k446">e6160c2f1ef79d759495ce28fe864a3aa18e5bbef4c5b2b5fbe989d3f5759371</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W058", "W094", "W112"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0154">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0154","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Chapter 9 used historical turnover research (\\autocite{W185} and \\autocite{W173}) to motivate preserving rationale and examined why AI-generated authorship weak","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Chapter 9 used historical turnover research (\autocite{W185} and \autocite{W173}) to motivate preserving rationale and examined why AI-generated authorship weak</data>
      <data key="k263">183</data>
      <data key="k265">183</data>
      <data key="k306">Chapter 9 used historical turnover research (\autocite{W185} and \autocite{W173}) to motivate preserving rationale and examined why AI-generated authorship weakens conventional knowledge proxies \autocite{W189}. Here we translate those concerns into a team policy. The system steward owns the dependency map and design rationale. The intent owner authorizes behavioral changes. The verification owner maintains independent oracles. The reviewer and release authority retain their decisions with source references.</data>
      <data key="k446">acb06e2954c9129c35ca272070d727a5fdc16292cf162dc3e2118b0793bb30df</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter11.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W173", "W185", "W189"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0155">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0155","kind":"cited_passage","label":"\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,height=.62\\textheight,keepaspectratio]{figures/jpg/26_Figure_11_1_Architect.jpg}\n\\caption{AI policies combine multiple governa","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
