    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0157">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0157","kind":"cited_passage","label":"A cost identity is useful: total observed cost equals discovery plus execution plus verification plus review plus integration and operations plus measured rewor","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">A cost identity is useful: total observed cost equals discovery plus execution plus verification plus review plus integration and operations plus measured rewor</data>
      <data key="k263">19</data>
      <data key="k265">19</data>
      <data key="k306">A cost identity is useful: total observed cost equals discovery plus execution plus verification plus review plus integration and operations plus measured rework and learning investment. Expected loss from defects can be added as a separately modeled risk term, provided probabilities and severities are labeled as assumptions or estimates. This is an engineering interpretation of \workposs{W095} reliability-and-cost proposal and \workposs{W145} DORA ROI guidance; neither provides a validated universal formula.</data>
      <data key="k446">7688c1826e877dc8282a1546b764d955d4e16ff445441a27d517450bce0463f7</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W095", "W145"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0158">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0158","kind":"cited_passage","label":"The production-stage differences examined in Chapter~\\ref{chap:02} make this economically consequential. Demirer, Musolff and Yang\\textquotesingle s observation","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">The production-stage differences examined in Chapter~\ref{chap:02} make this economically consequential. Demirer, Musolff and Yang\textquotesingle s observation</data>
      <data key="k263">45</data>
      <data key="k265">45</data>
      <data key="k306">The production-stage differences examined in Chapter~\ref{chap:02} make this economically consequential. Demirer, Musolff and Yang\textquotesingle s observational and structural analysis distinguishes coding activity from projects and releases; its estimates are not a forecast for this service. Use the endpoint relevant to the decision, and preserve the source-version limits in Appendix~\ref{app:methodology}. \autocite{W010}</data>
      <data key="k446">4aa9e399df08e1ebf221c78d8703d13e60f1987a0e1356a8afb8f8ac05a5d2b1</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W010"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0159">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0159","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Quality research demands estimator-level scrutiny. Agarwal and colleagues \\autocite{W001} stratify agent adoption by prior IDE use: complexity rises 34.85\\% and","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Quality research demands estimator-level scrutiny. Agarwal and colleagues \autocite{W001} stratify agent adoption by prior IDE use: complexity rises 34.85\% and</data>
      <data key="k263">47</data>
      <data key="k265">47</data>
      <data key="k306">Quality research demands estimator-level scrutiny. Agarwal and colleagues \autocite{W001} stratify agent adoption by prior IDE use: complexity rises 34.85\% and 42.87\% in two separate matched cohorts, not in a measured pooled 39\%-effect population. He and colleagues \autocite{W004} report preferred DiD warning and complexity effects of +30.26\% and +41.64\% in 806 Cursor adopters, but their Appendix Table~6 finds \emph{negative, non-significant} quality estimates under an alternative estimator. The two studies share part of their methodological lineage. Neither static warnings nor complexity establishes actual exploited defects, and such observational contrasts must not be pooled into a product-quality or safety guarantee.</data>
      <data key="k446">444dd747881e746e3d6f1a92de3aa2fe9a8a8e3e5990bb67092c2ea8c3fa751c</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W001", "W004"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0160">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0160","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Four modes are worth comparing: human-led execution, interactive pair assistance, a bounded agent producing a candidate for review, and a bounded agent followed","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Four modes are worth comparing: human-led execution, interactive pair assistance, a bounded agent producing a candidate for review, and a bounded agent followed</data>
      <data key="k263">61</data>
      <data key="k265">61</data>
      <data key="k306">Four modes are worth comparing: human-led execution, interactive pair assistance, a bounded agent producing a candidate for review, and a bounded agent followed by an independent checker. Their costs differ in developer minutes, inference, verifier effort, review time and expected rework. Their acceptance probabilities can also differ, but those probabilities must come from a comparable local pilot or remain explicit scenario assumptions. \autocite{W095} motivates this decision structure; \autocite{W028} supplies adoption context, while \autocite{W017} is a cross-domain experiment and cannot be counted as direct evidence of programming productivity.</data>
      <data key="k446">d873b5240a816d4e403565e9c6b46564177cf324223fa37be5232df36db312f0</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W017", "W028", "W095"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0161">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0161","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Cui and colleagues\\textquotesingle{} field experiments provide evidence about task output under a particular coding-assistant rollout. Chapter~\\ref{chap:02} pre","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Cui and colleagues\textquotesingle{} field experiments provide evidence about task output under a particular coding-assistant rollout. Chapter~\ref{chap:02} pre</data>
      <data key="k263">81</data>
      <data key="k265">81</data>
      <data key="k306">Cui and colleagues\textquotesingle{} field experiments provide evidence about task output under a particular coding-assistant rollout. Chapter~\ref{chap:02} presents the aggregate estimate and its uncertainty. A local economic model must additionally measure reviewer effort, released outcomes and maintenance obligations, rather than use the reported task gain as a lifetime-return multiplier. \autocite{W003}</data>
      <data key="k446">f06e8b0c4bc0fcc2096ff0eecf8c1c780e57f67d38c807f63540dcd6f3236881</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W003"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0162">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0162","kind":"cited_passage","label":"DORA\\textquotesingle s ROI guidance \\autocite{W145} is useful for structuring a business case, but scenario assumptions are not experimentally measured returns.","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">DORA\textquotesingle s ROI guidance \autocite{W145} is useful for structuring a business case, but scenario assumptions are not experimentally measured returns.</data>
      <data key="k263">84</data>
      <data key="k265">84</data>
      <data key="k306">DORA\textquotesingle s ROI guidance \autocite{W145} is useful for structuring a business case, but scenario assumptions are not experimentally measured returns. METR\textquotesingle s 2026 survey \autocite{W020} provides an example of why value and speed cannot be equated: 349 retained convenience-sample respondents reported about 1.4--2-fold median value increases across differently worded questions but around threefold perceived task-speed improvement. Their answers describe subjective counterfactuals and future expectations, not measured releases or accepted business outcomes. McKinsey\textquotesingle s twentyfold \emph{potential} agent-factory illustration \autocite{W028} likewise is not a verified ROI or a probability of acceptance. A local evaluation should compare modes on homogeneous tasks, retain failures and abandonment, and record reviewer effort and follow-up rework. Randomization is preferable where practical; staged rollout and matched comparisons require stronger assumptions.</data>
      <data key="k446">e445a9f197dd27ce15fa492ee5b345c8d18c87864c354ccb761d72c0da8df720</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W020", "W028", "W145"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0163">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0163","kind":"cited_passage","label":"A build-versus-buy story is useful only after its scope is made explicit. Orosz's account of replacing his own small testimonials service describes a bounded pe","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">A build-versus-buy story is useful only after its scope is made explicit. Orosz's account of replacing his own small testimonials service describes a bounded pe</data>
      <data key="k263">91</data>
      <data key="k265">91</data>
      <data key="k306">A build-versus-buy story is useful only after its scope is made explicit. Orosz's account of replacing his own small testimonials service describes a bounded personal implementation, not the lifetime cost of operating an equivalent service \oroszcite{P084}. The approval service has obligations after its first patch: policy updates, independent verification, replay guarantees, monitoring and a responsible owner. Feature similarity alone does not price those obligations.</data>
      <data key="k446">99c4a98e48e8ecac267e70814568af1a8c5df24513f4e0fe84ca1055168c008e</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["P084"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0164">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0164","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Simon Eskildsen's discussion of rough systems estimates, interviewed by Orosz, provides a complementary habit: challenge assumptions before accepting a benchmar","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Simon Eskildsen's discussion of rough systems estimates, interviewed by Orosz, provides a complementary habit: challenge assumptions before accepting a benchmar</data>
      <data key="k263">105</data>
      <data key="k265">93</data>
      <data key="k306">Simon Eskildsen's discussion of rough systems estimates, interviewed by Orosz, provides a complementary habit: challenge assumptions before accepting a benchmark at face value \oroszcite{P111}. Transferred here as an author-proposed analogy, rough arithmetic helps identify what to measure. It does not turn an estimate into pilot data.
\begin{center}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}&gt;{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}X&gt;{\RaggedRight\arraybackslash}X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Worksheet field} &amp; \textbf{Question before ranking modes} \\
\midrule
Service obligation and owner &amp; Do build, buy and assisted alternatives cover the same maintenance, policy and replay responsibilities? \\
Observed, assumed or unknown &amp; For each input record its unit, source and window. A scenario coefficient remains assumed; missing follow-up remains unknown. \\
Bound and next measurement &amp; Compare a rough effort/resource bound with the candidate estimate. Investigate the largest decision-sensitive gap rather than choosing the more attractive number. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}</data>
      <data key="k446">d72a17b1cdff2b28fbcbade0e3d14531ec0e62c3e11968b6ab57b810f725e1dc</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["P111"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0165">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0165","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Brynjolfsson, Chandar and Chen\\textquotesingle s Stanford Digital Economy Lab paper \\autocite{W125}, revised August 12, 2026, analyzes administrative payroll da","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Brynjolfsson, Chandar and Chen\textquotesingle s Stanford Digital Economy Lab paper \autocite{W125}, revised August 12, 2026, analyzes administrative payroll da</data>
      <data key="k263">118</data>
      <data key="k265">118</data>
      <data key="k306">Brynjolfsson, Chandar and Chen\textquotesingle s Stanford Digital Economy Lab paper \autocite{W125}, revised August 12, 2026, analyzes administrative payroll data covering millions of US workers through June 2026. It reports no evidence of widespread economy-wide displacement, but finds that employment for workers aged 22--25 in AI-exposed occupations is approximately 19\% below the trajectory implied by less-exposed peers. This is a relative comparison under the authors\textquotesingle{} model, not a 19\% national employment decline or proof that AI alone caused the gap. Earlier versions had different estimates and observation windows.</data>
      <data key="k446">0ad487d65adb2da05a036a48ee4fa3eac05067981d67e43b35764b685d6b2dfc</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W125"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0166">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0166","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Other mapped research on firm adoption \\autocite{W123}, Norwegian outcomes \\autocite{W128} and Finland \\autocite{W129} uses different populations and outcome me","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Other mapped research on firm adoption \autocite{W123}, Norwegian outcomes \autocite{W128} and Finland \autocite{W129} uses different populations and outcome me</data>
      <data key="k263">120</data>
      <data key="k265">120</data>
      <data key="k306">Other mapped research on firm adoption \autocite{W123}, Norwegian outcomes \autocite{W128} and Finland \autocite{W129} uses different populations and outcome measures. The primary ETLA Working Paper 135 examines Finnish earnings-registry and Labor Force Survey trends through September 2025, comparing the 22--25 and 26--30 age bands across occupational AI-exposure quantiles. Its Figures~3 and 7 show \emph{no persistent divergence} in youth employment by AI exposure, while Figure~8 finds no persistent exposure-based wage gaps. This is a geographically and temporally scoped null pattern, not proof of zero impacts or a randomized AI-adoption test. It is an important counterpoint to US hiring findings and cannot be averaged with job postings or AI-use surveys. \autocite{W129}</data>
      <data key="k446">ab303466065361292d68f4b55e434ac20015d56bf65ec321b57d23798afd1252</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W123", "W128", "W129"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0167">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0167","kind":"cited_passage","label":"The research literature must test alternative causes. Lambert and Schindler's June 2026 LSE Discussion Paper~2193 \\autocite{W131} links 243 million new hires an","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">The research literature must test alternative causes. Lambert and Schindler's June 2026 LSE Discussion Paper~2193 \autocite{W131} links 243 million new hires an</data>
      <data key="k263">132</data>
      <data key="k265">132</data>
      <data key="k306">The research literature must test alternative causes. Lambert and Schindler's June 2026 LSE Discussion Paper~2193 \autocite{W131} links 243 million new hires and 407 million job postings across the US, UK, Canada and Australia, 2017--2025. Separate exposure-gradient specifications associate two-standard-deviation increases in either generative-AI or work-from-home (WFH) exposure with about \textbf{five percentage points less junior hiring share} and \textbf{three points less low-experience vacancy share}. In jointly adjusted models, \emph{WFH remains predictive while the AI coefficient attenuates substantially and is often statistically insignificant} (abstract and specification discussion around printed pp.~12--13). Their continuous-exposure difference-in-differences requires an exposure-parallel-trends assumption; the result flags omitted-variable risk, not definitive proof that remote work caused all junior hiring change. Other job-posting counterpoints \autocite{W139,W140,W142} remain separate candidate explanations.</data>
      <data key="k446">38e1110c1f320d4a4fb3fb22266bf331e513222cbb234b637fae13e5a55f0d8a</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W131", "W139", "W140", "W142"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0168">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0168","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Garicano and Rayo model conditions under which apprenticeship remains viable as expertise and production technology change. The result is theoretical and depend","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Garicano and Rayo model conditions under which apprenticeship remains viable as expertise and production technology change. The result is theoretical and depend</data>
      <data key="k263">142</data>
      <data key="k265">142</data>
      <data key="k306">Garicano and Rayo model conditions under which apprenticeship remains viable as expertise and production technology change. The result is theoretical and depends on the model\textquotesingle s assumptions, not a measured threshold for a team\textquotesingle s training budget. Appendix~\ref{app:methodology} distinguishes the related manuscript versions. The practical question is how to preserve the learning work on which later expertise depends. \autocite{W119}</data>
      <data key="k446">301b79fa535c46f7b5a604fb0df9ac068fc47601954811a3932e1b9593882df7</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W119"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0169">
      <data key="k121">{"actual":[],"authors":"","category":"reader","collection":null,"id":"V10:TEXTREF:0169","kind":"cited_passage","label":"Ide \\autocite{W120} supplies a theoretical mechanism for learning-path disruption: automation can reduce novice--expert collaboration and future tacit knowledge","limitation":"","mapped":[],"printed":false,"streams":[],"summary":"","topics":[],"url":"","volumes":["V06"],"year":""}</data>
      <data key="k150">VERBATIM_BASELINE</data>
      <data key="k162">CLAIM</data>
      <data key="k246">cited_passage</data>
      <data key="k248">Ide \autocite{W120} supplies a theoretical mechanism for learning-path disruption: automation can reduce novice--expert collaboration and future tacit knowledge</data>
      <data key="k263">144</data>
      <data key="k265">144</data>
      <data key="k306">Ide \autocite{W120} supplies a theoretical mechanism for learning-path disruption: automation can reduce novice--expert collaboration and future tacit knowledge under specified equilibrium assumptions, with countervailing scale effects possible. A paper's theorem is not an estimated reduction in junior developer hiring. Humlum and Vestergaard \autocite{W136} instead use linked Danish adoption-survey and administrative outcomes; the March 2026 institutional working-paper revision reports small, precisely estimated short-run null wage and hours effects (the authors state effects above about two per cent can be excluded over the observed two-year period), alongside changes in task allocation. This audit inspected the March 13, 2026 RFBerlin original PDF beyond its abstract: the linked design samples approximately 25,000 workers at 7,000 workplaces in 11 exposed occupations; administrative outcomes run through December 2024. In Section 3, Equation (3) adds adopter-specific time trends and clusters standard errors at workplace--occupation level; Figure 5 reports that its dynamic earnings confidence intervals exclude changes larger than about 3\%, while the pooled specification in Figure 7(a) excludes larger than about 2\%. The two precision thresholds refer to \emph{different estimators}. Employer initiatives correlate with usage (approximately 41\% ever-use without initiatives versus 93\% with all three), which is descriptive and must not be read as randomized encouragement. The full coefficient tables and Statistics Denmark person-level microdata have \emph{not} been independently reconstructed here; the precision claims are verified as printed statements, not rerun regression results.</data>
      <data key="k446">1fdea1dc6003c4dc230f530897e5a1b2673a4fe5896f9cc62937e34c53656bd0</data>
      <data key="k463">chapters/chapter12.tex</data>
      <data key="k466">["W120", "W136"]</data>
      <data key="k487">CANONICAL_ID_AND_RECORDED_SCOPE_CHECKED_NOT_NEW_FULL_TEXT_VALIDATION</data>
      <data key="k513">V06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="V10:TEXTREF:0170">
